  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="8.4">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IIAtr">II Atr</ref> 8.4</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="8.4" corresp="#wit-q">Et ueniat, ubi primitus fuit interciatum.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="8.4" corresp="#wit-b">⁊ cume hit mon æure, ðær hit ærost befangen beo.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="8.4" corresp="#wit-b">⁊ tyme hit mon æure ðær hit ærost befangen beo.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b">And vouching to warranty shall always take place where the property has first been attached.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann">Und man ziehe es allemal dort zur Gewähr, wo es zuerst [vom Anefänger] gefasst worden ist.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">And let it always be vouched to warranty at the place where it was first attached [by the claimant].</ab>
        <note type="comparedBy" source="#wormald-mel" n="370"><ref target="clause:Forf_1">Forf 1</ref></note>
        <note type="editorial">B's ‘cume’ should be corrected to ‘tyme’ (B often writes c for t and u for y; Liebermann thinks the archetype already had this error).</note>
      </div>
    </body>
